  "question": "Let $y = x^{\\cos x}$. Using logarithmic differentiation, find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ and hence compute $\\frac{d^2 y}{dx^2}$ at $x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
  "answeroption": "D",
  "explanation": "Take log:<br /><br />$\\ln y=\\cos x \\ln x$.<br /><br />Differentiate:<br /><br />$\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\cos x}{x}-\\sin x \\ln x$.<br /><br />So,<br /><br />$\\frac{dy}{dx}=x^{\\cos x}\\left(\\frac{\\cos x}{x}-\\sin x \\ln x\\right)$.<br /><br />At $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$:<br /><br />$y=1$, $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\ln(\\frac{\\pi}{2})$.<br /><br />Differentiating again and substituting values gives:<br /><br />$\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}=\\left(\\ln(\\frac{\\pi}{2})\\right)^2-\\frac{4}{\\pi}$.",
  "explanation_image": [],
  "options": {
    "A": "$\\frac{dy}{dx}=x^{\\cos x}\\left(\\frac{\\sin x}{x}-\\cos x\\ln x\\right)$<br />$\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}\\bigg|_{x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}}=\\left(\\ln\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2$",
    "B": "$\\frac{dy}{dx}=x^{\\cos x}\\left(\\frac{\\cos x}{x}+\\sin x\\ln x\\right)$<br />$\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}\\bigg|_{x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}}=\\left(\\ln\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2+\\frac{4}{\\pi}$",
    "C": "$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\cos x\\,x^{\\cos x-1}$<br />$\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}\\bigg|_{x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}}=-\\frac{4}{\\pi}$",
    "D": "$\\frac{dy}{dx}=x^{\\cos x}\\left(\\frac{\\cos x}{x}-\\sin x\\ln x\\right)$<br />$\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}\\bigg|_{x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}}=\\left(\\ln\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{4}{\\pi}$"
